+  <section class="about" aria-labelledby="about-title">
+    <h2 id="about-title" class="section">Why AEOLUS</h2>
+    <div class="features">
+      <article class="feature">
+        <h3>Accuracy without the cost per geometry</h3>
+        <p>Density Functional Theory is fast but can be unreliable, particularly for electron correlation. FermiNet solves the electronic Schr&ouml;dinger equation directly and is highly accurate, but it needs a full re-optimization for every molecular geometry.</p>
+      </article>
+      <article class="feature">
+        <h3>Geometry-conditioned GNN layer</h3>
+        <p>AEOLUS extends FermiNet with an E(3)-equivariant graph neural network that injects geometry-aware embeddings into the electron representation, so one trained model can evaluate energies across a continuous space of related geometries.</p>
+      </article>
+      <article class="feature">
+        <h3>Physical guarantees preserved</h3>
+        <p>Fermionic antisymmetry, rotational and translational equivariance, and correct electron-nucleus cusp behavior are kept, while the cost of evaluating many conformers drops.</p>
+      </article>
+      <article class="feature">
+        <h3>Stability ranking</h3>
+        <p>The output is a tool for ranking the relative stability of small-molecule conformers and tautomers at near-chemical accuracy.</p>
+      </article>
+      <article class="feature">
+        <h3>Validation and benchmarks</h3>
+        <p>Validated against coupled-cluster (CCSD(T)) reference calculations, with QM7 for baseline correctness and GEOM and Platinum for conformer-specific evaluation.</p>
+      </article>
+      <article class="feature">
+        <h3>A precision filtering step</h3>
+        <p>Applied to a shortlist of promising candidates, not as a replacement for existing discovery pipelines, to reduce wasted synthesis effort on unstable structures.</p>
+      </article>
+    </div>
+
+    <ul class="sdg" aria-label="Sustainable Development Goals">
+      <li>SDG 9: Industry, innovation and infrastructure</li>
+      <li>SDG 3: Good health and well-being</li>
+      <li>SDG 12: Responsible consumption and production</li>
+    </ul>
+
+    <p class="status"><strong>Project status.</strong> AEOLUS is in early development. The viewer below shows the first milestone, a hydrogen atom trained with FermiNet. The geometry-conditioned GNN, the conformer ranking and the benchmarks described above are planned work.</p>
+
+    <details class="abstract">
+      <summary>Read the full abstract</summary>
+      <p>Modern drug discovery and materials design depend on computational methods to evaluate molecular stability before committing to costly laboratory synthesis. Researchers currently face a fundamental tradeoff: Density Functional Theory (DFT) is fast but can be unreliable, particularly in capturing electron correlation, while neural-network-based quantum Monte Carlo methods such as FermiNet solve the electronic Schr&ouml;dinger equation directly, achieving high accuracy but requiring full re-optimization for every individual molecular geometry. This makes FermiNet accurate yet impractically slow when applied across the many conformers and tautomers a single candidate molecule can adopt.</p>
+      <p>AEOLUS addresses this gap by extending FermiNet with a geometry-conditioned, E(3)-equivariant Graph Neural Network layer. Rather than training a separate model for each configuration, AEOLUS injects geometry-aware embeddings into FermiNet's electron representation, enabling a single trained model to evaluate energies across a continuous space of related molecular geometries. This preserves FermiNet's core physical guarantees &mdash; fermionic antisymmetry, rotational and translational equivariance, and correct electron-nucleus cusp behavior &mdash; while significantly reducing the computational cost of evaluating multiple conformers.</p>
+      <p>The core output is a tool for ranking the relative stability of small-molecule conformers and tautomers at near-chemical accuracy, validated against coupled-cluster (CCSD(T)) reference calculations and benchmarked using QM7 for baseline correctness and GEOM/Platinum for conformer-specific evaluation. AEOLUS is positioned not as a replacement for existing discovery pipelines, but as a precision filtering step applied to a shortlist of promising candidates, reducing wasted synthesis effort on unstable structures.</p>
+      <p>By advancing scientific computational infrastructure (SDG 9), supporting pharmaceutical research and development (SDG 3), and reducing resource waste in early-stage research (SDG 12), AEOLUS contributes to more efficient, sustainable, and accessible scientific discovery.</p>
+    </details>
+  </section>
+
